// The first index that matched, folded through a scan -- and the word-list
// setter, which has nowhere better to live.
//
// `id` has no `break` and no early return, so a search is a loop that visits
// every element and keeps the first hit. That fold is the same three lines
// wherever it appears, and the compiler rejects a second copy of it.
//
// `at` is the answer so far (-1 for none) and `hit` is whether element `i`
// matched. The `at < 0` test is what makes it *first* rather than last.
//
// Lifted from idem/engine/core/util/lst/w/pick.id and w/wset.id.

lst_pick(int at, int i, int hit) {
  int v = at;
  if (hit = 1 && at < 0) {
    v = i;
  }
} return int v;
(-1, 3, 1):(3)
(5, 3, 1):(5)

// The same write as lset, for a list of words. `id` has no generics, so a list
// of 64-bit values needs its own setter -- an imported list cannot be
// index-assigned directly, whatever it holds.
wset(word[] ws, int i, word wv) {
  ws[i] = wv;
} return void;
([1000000000000, 2], 0, 5):([5, 2], 0, 5)
([1, 2], 1, 9999999999):([1, 9999999999], 1, 9999999999)
